# Where the ferry town's places stand to each other, on the town plan.
lesson language-next-to v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=2 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Next to the museum"
  goal "Say where places are in [[language.name]]: next to, opposite, between, near and at the end of the street."

  look {
    scene 28x23 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        townplan plan places=["[[town.the-cafe]]", "[[town.the-shop]]", "[[town.the-park]]", "[[town.the-beach]]", "[[town.the-museum]]", "[[town.the-station]]", "[[town.the-square]]"]
      }
    }
    say """
      To say where something is: [[town.next-to]] is next to, [[town.opposite-2]] is opposite,
      [[town.between]] is between, [[town.near-2]] is near, [[town.far-from]] is far from, and
      [[town.at-the-end-of-the-street]] is at the end of the street. On the plan, the café and the
      shop face each other across the road: the shop [[town.it-is-opposite-the-cafe]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.next-to-name k=3
    }
    show language.next-to-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.next-to-meaning k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.next-to-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.next-to-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.next-to-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.next-to-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.next-to-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.next-to-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.next-to-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-next-to-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.next-to-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[town.next-to]], next to; [[town.opposite-2]], opposite; [[town.between]], between; [[town.at-the-end-of-the-street]], at the end of the street."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is next to and opposite swapped, and between used for only one
    thing. Every clue in the naming questions points to one building on the plan, so ask the child
    to put a finger on each place a clue names. The two-star question is about how the language
    joins its little words. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word
    typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as
    Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, set out three cups as buildings and
    move a toy next to, opposite and between them, saying each. Off the page, describe where things
    are in your kitchen in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3
    to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and actions orally and in
    writing"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can talk about familiar and simple matters
    in daily life by using simple words and phrases and basic expressions"; and CEFR A2, "Can
    describe in simple terms aspects of his/her background, immediate environment and matters in
    areas of immediate need". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[town.the-station]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[town.the-shop]] and [[town.the-square]] and the words to write are [[town.opposite]] and [[town.between]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[town.the-beach]] and [[town.the-museum]] and the words to write are [[town.end]], [[town.behind]] and [[town.far]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
